  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Unsettled pattern will linger through this\nweekend across eastern NC as upper trough continues to linger\nover the eastern CONUS and stalled front sits to our north and\nwest. Ahead of the frontal boundary, a deep plume of Gulf\nmoisture continues to stream northeastward along the coast of\nthe Carolinas, characterized by PWATs of 2\"+ per mesoanalysis.\nAs is typical overnight, focus of convection is waning over the\nAlbemarle Peninsula with loss of heating while activity begins\nto blossom offshore.\n\nMain focus in the near term is development of low stratus and\nsome patchy fog with very weak winds and saturated boundary\nlayer. Observations are already showing some spotty development\nacross the inner coastal plain and expect this to expand as we\nnear dawn, with highest probabilities of low clouds west of\nHighway 17.\n\nLater today, this will play a role in the amount of convective\ncoverage. Stalled front will remain in the area and provide a \npotential focal point for development and upper troughing will\nlinger (although it is forecast to begin lifting out of the area\nlater in the day, limiting overall coverage). However, like\nyesterday, with very weak flow lower clouds will likely take\ntheir time eroding and could significantly hamper overall\ninstability. NBM probabilities for rainfall are likely too\naggressive and capped at high-end chance for now. Best chances\nfor more robust convection will likely be farther north along\nthe edge of the stratus deck where differential heating zones\nwill develop - this favors areas along and north of Highway 264\nparticularly in the afternoon and evening. Given weak shear, a\nstrong storm is unlikely but deep moisture continues to point to\na torrential rainfall threat. WPC has retained our area in a\nMarginal (Level 1 of 4) risk of excessive rainfall for today to\naccount for this. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Shower and thunderstorm coverage gradually wanes\nnext week as upper ridging builds into the region from the west.\nCannot completely rule out an isolated shower or storm through\nthe period along sea and sound breezes although activity will\nlikely be short-lived. The main story will be the building heat\nwith highs expected to climb into the 90s early next week and \npeaking in the mid to upper 90s as we move into the middle of \nthe week. Heat headlines may be needed as early as Wednesday\nwith forecast heat indices climbing above 105, especially along\nthe coast and inland counties along and south of US70.",
